  "notes": "UK deep-house and garage-house crossover, and the entry names the differentiator outright: the kit is \"a four-square kick with a soft attack, clap or rimshot on 2&4, offbeat open hats, and a shuffled 16th shaker — the swing is the parameter that separates his records from the big-room field\". So `swing.amount` is 0.56 against the lane's straight 0.50, the snare carries a rimshot layer, the kick attack is soft (the quietest accent tier of the house-facing producers here) and the shaker runs 16ths. The second stated fact is that \"the bass is the primary groove element\" — \"a syncopated organ-style or plucked sub-bass playing a walking line between chord tones\" — which is a 0.40 root probability, the second-lowest in this section, with five passing tones and a high anticipation; the entry even says \"countermelody is the bass\", which is why the counter sits an octave below the lead. Harmony is \"7th and 9th deep-house voicings and an organ-bass relationship borrowed from 1990s house\", marked `[LOW-CONF]` there and repeated here. Loops are 4 bars and \"the hook is often a repeated one-bar vocal cell\". ⚠ Every velocity, density and register above is INFERRED; the research calls the parameters deep-house/garage-house convention.",
